  "worked_solution": "The addition rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(x)={f}^{\u2032}(x)+{g}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(3)={f}^{\u2032}(3)+{g}^{\u2032}(3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2,4)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2.5,4)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/19a32fa6a7b2b6481052ab3539838bb00d486f92ea3f08b54efa2a1cfc69d221.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u0394f}{\u0394x}=\\frac{0-2}{4-2}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(3)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2.5,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u0394g}{\u0394x}=\\frac{1-1}{4-2.5}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{\u2032}(3)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(3) & ={f}^{\u2032}(3)+{g}^{\u2032}(3) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
